DESCRIPTION:Fall Ballet\nWhite Shadows\nChristian Claessens\n Longtime Jac
 obs faculty member Christian Claessens teams up with the Jacobs Percussion
  Department to create this world premiere based on the last three movement
 s of Steve Reich’s Sextet\, employing marimbas\, vibraphones\, bass drum
 s\, and more. The eight dancers embody movement from pulsations to melodic
  patterns to rhythmic ambiguity.\n Christian Claessens\, Choreographer\n J
 oseph Gramley\, Conductor and Chamber Music Coach\n Blake Kile\, Lighting 
 Designer\n Valse-Fantaisie\nGeorge Balanchine\n Attended by their single p
 artner\, five ballerinas move in a whirl of perpetual motion to Mikhail Gl
 inka’s waltzing rhythms. Although it was popularly called “the Melanch
 oly Waltz\,” this 1967 rendering is fast and light and was originally pr
 esented as the second section of Glinkiana\, which featured former IU Dist
 inguished Professor Violette Verdy in its New York City Ballet premiere.\n
  George Balanchine\, Choreographer\n Kyra Nichols\, Stager\n Blake Kile\, 
 Lighting Designer\n Dignity in Restraint\nCaili Quan\n Born to a Chamorro 
 Filipino family in Guam\, Caili Quan created this punchy work on New York 
 City Ballet dancers to Beethoven’s String Quartet No. 12. Her love for 
 “showing people the music” is on full display as she corresponds five 
 major chords to the five core emotions of anger\, fear\, disgust\, joy\, a
 nd sadness.\n Caili Quan\, Choreographer\n Andrea Yorita\, Stager\n Blake 
 Kile\, Lighting Designer\n World Premiere\nJames Whiteside\n A principal d
 ancer and choreographer with American Ballet Theatre\, James Whiteside is 
 also a pop music performer\, model\, drag queen\, podcast host\, and autho
 r. He brings his wildly eclectic style to Jacobs for several weeks as he c
 reates his latest work\, with the students of the Ballet Department.\n Jam
