BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Future Tense 2026
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T160000
UID:442b7779de2e43337fb9e5e88207e733@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/67b8
 488/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1920x1536+0+0/resize/254x203!/quality/90/?u
 rl=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F50%2F68%2Fd9a7e48247dfb7
 8b479749e07c8e%2Fallanson-audrey-cb.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Grunwald Gallery of Art\nThe Grunwald Gallery of 
 Art at Indiana University Bloomington partners with Surface Design Associa
 tion to present "Future Tense 2026"—a national student exhibition juried
  by José Santiago Pérez. Since 2017\, Surface Design Association (SDA) h
 as invited students to submit work for the annual Juried Student Exhibitio
 n. The ninth iteration of this exhibition will be held at Indiana Universi
 ty’s Grunwald Gallery of Art in Bloomington\, IN\, from August 25-Octobe
 r 31\, 2026. Exhibiting artists are eligible for four awards recognizing e
 xcellence in artistic practice. \n "Future Tense” celebrates the creativ
 e work of student artists\, designers\, and makers working with\, or inspi
 red by fiber\, or textile materials\, or techniques. This exhibition offer
 s a glimpse into the future of contemporary fibers by presenting the very 
 best work being made by students in the field today. \n Opening reception:
  Friday\, September 4\, 6-8 pm\, Grunwald Gallery\n Juror's Remarks and Aw
 ards: Friday\, September 4\, 6:30 pm\, Grunwald Gallery\n Regular Gallery 
 Hours: Tuesday - Saturday\, 12 pm - 4 pm
LOCATION:Grunwald Gallery of Art\, 1201 E Seventh Street\, Bloomington\, I
 ndiana 47405
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/future-tense-2026-09-09-2
 026-15-02-14
X-SOURCE:WFIU Community Calendar
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Story: Sajik Kim
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T160000
UID:3dc0b4198f77d34ce912774a18bfb547@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/d4d3
 349/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1080x1350+0+0/resize/254x318!/quality/90/?u
 rl=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F0b%2F90%2Fa7fdae7042f2af
 9c569b86812bbb%2Fthe-girl-who-dreams-with-seven-birds.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Grunwald Gallery of Art\nCo-curated by Osamu Jame
 s Nakagawa (Ruth N. Halls Distinguished Professor of Photography) and Lind
 a Tien (Director of the Grunwald Gallery)\, photography exhibition STORY i
 s a creation myth drawn from the lived experience of Zainichi artist Sajik
  Kim\, born and raised in Japan as a third-generation Korean national. Inf
 used with theatrical elements and embodied performance\, STORY explores ho
 w one might establish a sense of self and live with rootedness amid the co
 nstant flux of identity. \n Directed by Kim herself\, STORY poses the ques
 tion: What is homeland? Every nation and people possess a creation myth th
 at narrates the beginning of their community and sustains their collective
  identity. Yet\, for Zainichi artist Kim\, born and raised in Japan as a t
 hird-generation Korean national\, there was a persistent sense of not bein
 g able to fully embody a fixed ethnic identity. From this position\, Kim a
 ttempts to weave a personal “creation myth” drawn from her lived exper
 ience. \n In developing this series\, Kim conducted research within Japan\
 , incorporating elements from everyday life—such as food\, clothing\, ho
 using\, education\, and festivals as well as encounters with people and hi
 storical events. STORY thus explores how one might establish a sense of se
 lf and live with rootedness amid the constant flux of identity. Furthermor
 e\, it seeks to resonate with and offer insight to those who\, in an incre
 asingly complex world\, must navigate similarly unstable identities. \n Em
 ploying the methodology of staged photography\, the STORY series is orches
 trated through comprehensive direction—including lighting\, makeup\, cos
 tume\, and posing. While photographic in form\, the works are simultaneous
 ly infused with theatrical elements and embodied performance. \n Artist Ta
 lk: Friday\, September 11\, 12-1 pm\, Shreve Auditorium-Global and Interna
 tional Studies Building (355 N. Eagleson Avenue)\n Opening reception: Frid
 ay\, September 11\, 6-8 pm\, Grunwald Gallery\n Artist Performance: Friday
 \, September 11\, 7 pm\, Grunwald Gallery
LOCATION:Grunwald Gallery of Art\, 1201 E Seventh Street\, Bloomington\, I
 ndiana 47405
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/story-sajik-kim-09-09-202
 6-15-05-30
X-SOURCE:WFIU Community Calendar
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Adult Ukulele Class
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T183000
UID:cba9da954a87d45b7ea17468722bf22a@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/04a9
 41f/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1080x1920+0+0/resize/254x452!/quality/90/?u
 rl=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fac%2Fe8%2Fe40945974fc18e
 4570b7d96936cc%2Face-ukulele-class-flyer.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Presented by The Arts Federation\nJoin us for a fun\, 8-week c
 lass designed for adults who are new to the ukulele\nor looking to build a
  strong foundation.\nNo prior musical experience is required. Bring your o
 wn ukulele or lend\none from TAF at no cost!\nIn this class\, you'll:\n•
  Learn foundational ukulele skills and basic chords\n• Explore beginner-
 friendly songs and playing techniques\n• Learn the basics of music theor
 y\n• Build confidence playing the ukulele\n Instructor: Olivia Sutherlin
 \nOlivia Sutherlin is a multidisciplinary musician with a lifelong backgro
 und in\nthe performing arts\, including theater\, choir\, and band.\n Loca
