BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Story: Sajik Kim
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261112T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261112T160000
UID:e9f30c2d3a194f35ac9976f1d132f237@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/d4d3
 349/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1080x1350+0+0/resize/254x318!/quality/90/?u
 rl=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F0b%2F90%2Fa7fdae7042f2af
 9c569b86812bbb%2Fthe-girl-who-dreams-with-seven-birds.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Grunwald Gallery of Art\nCo-curated by Osamu Jame
 s Nakagawa (Ruth N. Halls Distinguished Professor of Photography) and Lind
 a Tien (Director of the Grunwald Gallery)\, photography exhibition STORY i
 s a creation myth drawn from the lived experience of Zainichi artist Sajik
  Kim\, born and raised in Japan as a third-generation Korean national. Inf
 used with theatrical elements and embodied performance\, STORY explores ho
 w one might establish a sense of self and live with rootedness amid the co
 nstant flux of identity. \n Directed by Kim herself\, STORY poses the ques
 tion: What is homeland? Every nation and people possess a creation myth th
 at narrates the beginning of their community and sustains their collective
  identity. Yet\, for Zainichi artist Kim\, born and raised in Japan as a t
 hird-generation Korean national\, there was a persistent sense of not bein
 g able to fully embody a fixed ethnic identity. From this position\, Kim a
 ttempts to weave a personal “creation myth” drawn from her lived exper
 ience. \n In developing this series\, Kim conducted research within Japan\
 , incorporating elements from everyday life—such as food\, clothing\, ho
 using\, education\, and festivals as well as encounters with people and hi
 storical events. STORY thus explores how one might establish a sense of se
 lf and live with rootedness amid the constant flux of identity. Furthermor
 e\, it seeks to resonate with and offer insight to those who\, in an incre
 asingly complex world\, must navigate similarly unstable identities. \n Em
 ploying the methodology of staged photography\, the STORY series is orches
 trated through comprehensive direction—including lighting\, makeup\, cos
 tume\, and posing. While photographic in form\, the works are simultaneous
 ly infused with theatrical elements and embodied performance. \n Artist Ta
 lk: Friday\, September 11\, 12-1 pm\, Shreve Auditorium-Global and Interna
 tional Studies Building (355 N. Eagleson Avenue)\n Opening reception: Frid
 ay\, September 11\, 6-8 pm\, Grunwald Gallery\n Artist Performance: Friday
 \, September 11\, 7 pm\, Grunwald Gallery
LOCATION:Grunwald Gallery of Art\, 1201 E Seventh Street\, Bloomington\, I
 ndiana 47405
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/story-sajik-kim-09-09-202
 6-15-05-30
X-SOURCE:WFIU Community Calendar
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Curiosity Lab at WonderLab
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261112T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261112T174500
UID:d721eed51d6a8123110d950765bba788@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/b1a7
 0e2/2147483647/strip/true/crop/801x801+0+0/resize/254x254!/quality/90/?url
 =http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F62%2F89%2Ff6d444d54138a4b0
 377124b045ba%2Fcl-logo-web-info-2.png
DESCRIPTION:Presented by WonderLab Science Museum\nCuriosity Lab is an aft
 er-school\, free play program for kids ages 6-10 years. Children are invit
 ed to explore\, experiment\, and engage in hands-on sensory activities at 
 their own pace. No instructions\, no pressure—just curiosity-driven play
 . Held in WonderLab (or sometimes outdoors weather permitting)\, the space
  is set up for exploration with a variety of rotating materials and statio
 ns. \n There will be one main activity/installment/manipulative for the mo
 nth that will be brought out every Thursday. This will rotate with the mon
 th and the month's theme.\nTickets: https://wonderlab.org/
LOCATION:Wonderlab\, 308 W 4th St\, Bloomington\, Indiana 47401
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/curiosity-lab-at-wonderla
 b-22-07-2026-13-42-23
X-SOURCE:WFIU Community Calendar
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Go Beyond 2026
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261112T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261112T220000
UID:6c46db3987aa24e0e1379d0003e195ae@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/d281
 364/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1275x1875+0+0/resize/254x374!/quality/90/?u
 rl=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F19%2F0b%2Fb70063f241cc8b
 7b157b9edc80bf%2Fcg-event-postcard-templatefinal1.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Common Ground Films\nGo Beyond is a benefit dinne
 r raising the final funds to send blind Navy veteran Lonnie Bedwell to the
  North Pole\, the last leg of his Explorers Grand Slam.\n Every ticket and
  sponsorship helps complete Beyond Vision\, the feature documentary chroni
 cling his journey from losing his sight in 1997 to conquering all seven su
 mmits.\n Join us for cocktails\, dinner\, a filmmaker panel\, a silent auc
 tion\, and an exclusive first look at scenes from the film\, all in celebr
 ation of a story that proves no summit is out of reach.\nTickets: https://
 www.commongroundfilms.org/gobeyond
LOCATION:The Woolery Mill\, 2250 W Sunstone Dr\, Bloomington\, Indiana 474
 03
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/go-beyond-2026-21-08-2026
 -09-43-17
